      "text": "A rainbow is a spectacular natural phenomenon that occurs when sunlight interacts with water droplets in the atmosphere, creating a spectrum of colors that we see in the sky. This optical effect is a complex interplay of physics involving refraction, reflection, dispersion, and the viewing angle. To understand how a rainbow forms, one must first consider the properties of light and the behavior of water droplets.\n\nThe process begins with the entry of sunlight into a water droplet. Sunlight is composed of all visible wavelengths of the electromagnetic spectrum, ranging from red to violet. As these rays enter the droplet, they slow down and bend, a process known as refraction. Crucially, different wavelengths of light refract at slightly different angles. Red light has the longest wavelength and bends the least, while violet light has the shortest wavelength and bends the most. This separation of colors is called dispersion. Upon entering the droplet, the light ray bends toward the normal line (an imaginary line perpendicular to the surface).\n\nInside the droplet, the light undergoes a second refraction as it exits the back of the droplet. However, the path is not entirely linear; the light ray undergoes a total internal reflection at the back of the droplet before emerging. This reflection is",
